A||N||29||Slapnutz2000||Pioneer 4x10 install||||5||
Z||000000||Slapnutz2000||01-07-2001||01:49 PM||||I know the clips are different on the Pioneer 4x10s in relation to the stock speakers. Can I get clip adapters from Crutchfield and how much do they cost? Also, do the new clip adapters just snap on or do I have to solder? I HATE soldering!||||reg||5
Z||000001||MrPBody||01-07-2001||02:39 PM||||You can remove the connectors from your original speakers.  Pry up the plastic latch, and they slip off.  Clip the braided leads close to the speaker cone.  I don't know how Pioneers connect.  You may have to solder.  (Sorry.)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||01-07-2001||03:41 PM||||The adapters from Crutchfield just clip into the fiero harness and then there are spade plugs to clip on the Pioneer speakers. Takes about 1 minute per speaker.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||01-07-2001||04:03 PM||||I took my OE connectors off my dead speakers as mentioned above. I got a pack of 6 crimp on ends from radish rack (radio shack). there are 2 each of 3 sizes common to speakers.<P>I used some plastic tubing to protect the wires and crimped on the ends. Technically you want them the same polarity as the original speakers. In reallity it doesn't seem to matter much how you make them as long as both are the same. Because of the placement relative the driver/passenger the speakers are always out of phase by the time the sound actually gets to you.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||01-07-2001||04:15 PM||||If using a coaxial speaker like a Pioneer the + and - sides are very important. Most coaxial type of speakers use a capacitor to block off the lower freqs going to the tweeters. With the polarity backwards the polarity of the capacitor (it is an electrolytic cap so it is polarized) is on the wrong side of the speaker. This is what usually causes the tweeters to fail since the low freqs will get to the voice coil and overheat it. On GM wiring the lighter of the 2 wires is the ground side (yellow, blue, tan, and light green) Darker colors are positive side. (brown, dark blue, gray/black stripe, and dark green)<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 01-07-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000005||Slapnutz2000||01-07-2001||04:39 PM||||Thanks guys! With all the help I'm getting from the forum, my little 2m4 is finally getting to look like I envisioned.<P>One more thing. I'm planning on buying a Pioneer DEH-45dh CD player. Since I'm miserable at soldering - could someone solder the Pioneer harness to a Crutchfield harness so I can make the system plug and play? I already bought a wiring harness for my Fiero from Crutchfield made for that specific model.<P>Thanks again everyone!||||reg||10
Z||000006||286SE'S||01-08-2001||02:00 PM||||When I bought my Pioneer speakers from cruchfield they gave me the adapters for the speakers for free. They also gave me the GM adapter when I bought a stereo from them. You are right that the adapter must be soldered to the Pioneer connector's wires, then covered with shrink tubing (tape WILL fall off) Even with Crutchfields helpfull notes it was not straightforward which wires went to what. Subwoofer never worked again but since the speaker in it was blown it was no big loss.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||01-08-2001||02:09 PM||||286SE's ~ The stock subwoofer isn't "plug and play" You need to hook the 2 pink wires to the remote line to power the amp. Usually we replace the speaker in the sub and use an aftermarket amp. The problem is it eliminates the use of the slider switch on the light panel above.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Rednax Asbestos||01-08-2001||03:00 PM||||Hi oreif,<P>Please correct me if i'm wrong but the + from a speaker is ,compared to the negative, a AC current. So the cappacitor "sees" both a positive and negative voltage. The wiring is important to prevent phase diverences between speakers.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||01-08-2001||03:46 PM||||The capacitor works like a filter. It allows lower frequencies to pass thru to the negative side and not go thru the voice coil. It is not relying on the voltage itself just the frequency that is on it. A capacitor can be used both ways. Electrolytic capacitors have a better isolation in filtering out unwanted frequencies. A mechanical tuner is basically a variable capacitor. You tune it to the freq you want to pass thru. <BR>BTW ~ It is a sine wave at a DC level current flows in one direction. AC is a sinewave that goes negative and positive. 120vac if read with a DC meter cycles form +60V to -60V. Peak to Peak is 120V. The currenet travels in 2 directions.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 01-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||01-08-2001||08:19 PM||||Unless I missed something..... <P>Yes the crossover caps are electrolytic types. However they are specifically made for this application and are termed "non polar." No, I never really got into the construction of them beyond that, I haven't built any crossovers in years. You can't use regular electrolytics to do it. They will die in fairly short order. I have never seen a cap on a car speaker die no matter what you hook it up to unless the thing was over driven.<P>If you measure the amp output relative the chasis ground you'll get a DC biased AC wave form. If you measure it with a scope across the speaker leads you'll usually see a true zero crossing AC. The voltage may not drop below chasis ground but it is still AC to the speaker's point of view. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Oreif||01-08-2001||11:01 PM||||The output finals of most amplifiers are +5v and -5v. This is not an AC signal. The current isn't alternating the current is only going 1 direction. A sine wave does not mean AC voltage. The frequency is the only thing that varies. The peak to peak level is only varied by the power driven out. I.E. the current is controlled by the level of power out. Electrolytics are used in most automotive type of speakers due to the lower impedence of the voice coils and the higher range of operating temps. (winter cold summer heat) Most that I have seen are polarized. Home speakers have a higher impedence voice coil and do not need the electrolytics. I have built many custom crossovers for auto and home. Depending on requirements you can build the with 6db, 12db, 18db, and 24db crossovers. Electrolytics are commonly used for low impedence, low voltage, high environment (temperature). They are not the most accurate but they are durable.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 01-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||rodmcneill||01-08-2001||11:14 PM||||I just love it when these two guys(Oreif,Theogre) get going. It is like 2 college professors arguing, if only I could understand...||||reg||1
Z||000013||Rednax Asbestos||01-09-2001||04:05 PM||||Oreif, it seems to me that a speaker coil needs to have an alternating current so it can create a two way motion along it's axis. The way you discribe it it can only move in one direction. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Oreif||01-09-2001||06:08 PM||||The current does not alternate direction. The voice coil creates a magnetic field. The strength of the field determines cone movement. The field strength is increased and decreased by the amplitude of the sine wave of the frequency. This pushes the cone forward (since it is an opposite polarity than the magnet on the back of the speaker it pushes away from it) and the suspension of the cone pulls it back at the bottom of the sinewave (less voltage drive/ magnetism). This cycle is at the at the specific frequency of the sinewave and the vibration is the sound. A speaker with + and - hooked up properly pushes the cone outward. If you were to reverse the lines the speaker would be pulled inward at the peak of the sinewave (same polarity as magnet on back so it would attract to it). <BR>Basically a speaker voice coil is an electro-magnet that the strength is controlled by the voltage traveling thru it. Since it is by another magnet it will repel or attract depending on how it is phased. The more voltage the stronger the magnetic force is, less voltage less force. The cone size and voice coil size determine which frequencies are reproduced efficiently. (sp?) Smaller cones can move faster than large cones so they reproduce higher freq's better. Larger cones can push more air which is required for very low freq's.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">   <P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 01-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Indiana_resto_guy||01-09-2001||06:22 PM||||So Oreif, what your saying in a nutshell is that with the capicator installed, it works similar to a low voltage band-pass filter, without the rest of the 'typical' circuitry. Right?||||reg||1
Z||000016||stsmithgt||01-09-2001||06:32 PM||||Wow all this talk about inductive and capacitive reactance talk.  I had to stop soldering to get a better line on what you guys are talking about.  I haven't looked into crossovers much, but don't they use inductors to filter the lower frequencies and capacitors to filter the higher frequencies (polarized) because they perform better that way?  It's like the inductor would get too hot?, with high frequencies or their flux wave reacting time isn't efficiant enough at that speed? and caps would pop from being over driven at low frequencies.  I could be off, but that's my impression as simple as it may be.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Oh well, I'll continue to read instead of type.||||reg||6
Z||000017||Oreif||01-09-2001||06:34 PM||||Yes, The rest of the typical circuitry is for increasing roll off slope. A cap by itself is about a 6DB roll-off. Most home and electronic crossovers are 12db to 18db of roll-off. (some very high end stuff even go to 24db!) The roll off is how many db's the it drops per hertz. So with a 18db crossover set at 100hz the sound is 18db quieter at 101hz. At 102hz it is at 36db down. This gives the speaker less distortion and a better transition between 2 speakers. (Two speakers putting ot the same freq 6db down would add distortion to the sound.)<BR>stsmithgt ~ Yes inductors are better for lower freq's We are talking about the caps used on coaxial speakers (higher freq's) but yes inductors are way better than caps at the lower freq's<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 01-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||DJRice||01-09-2001||10:24 PM||||So what your saying is that the little barrel looking thing keeps the boom from killin the tweets?<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000019||Oreif||01-09-2001||11:01 PM||||(LOL) Yes the "barrel thingy" keeps everything from about 5000hz and below out of the tweets.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Rednax Asbestos||01-10-2001||05:10 PM||||I'm sorry Oreif but i an not convinced yet.<P>your english is ofcours a lot better then mine (I'm dutch) so I'm having a bit difficulty explaining this. but.<P>A speaker does not rely on it's suspension to move the cone back to it's origonal position. the amplifier drives the + terminal and keeps the - terminal on a constant level. this means that the amplifier has to put out a positive AND negative voltage on the terminal according to the music. It is difficult for an amplifier to accuratly folow a sinewave around the 0 volt point. This is exactly what high-end amplifiers try to avoid by raising the voltage on the whole system, so the alternation does not occur around the 0 volt point but on a higher  voltage (40 volt or something like that).<P>perhaps this link will clarify: <A HREF="http://www.sound.au.com/class-a.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.sound.au.com/class-a.htm</A> <P>looking forward to your reply.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Rednax Asbestos (edited 01-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||Oreif||01-10-2001||07:19 PM||||Yes an amplifiers output is a positive and negative voltage. But the current only flows in one direction. The negative side of a speaker is lower with respect to the positive side. So even though the sinewave is at a +5v and -5v the negative side of the speaker is at a lower potential. The speaker is part of the amplifier circuit and the reference that the amplifier uses is the same as the negative side of the speaker. As for the sinewave crossing "0" volts they are talking about 0 equal to the negative reference. If the sinewave gets near the negative reference the bottom part of the signal will clip. If you try to drive more into the amplifer final that is greater than the output, You would then clip the top and bottom portions of the signal. (clipping top and/or bottom causes distortion).||||reg||1
Z||000022||Rednax Asbestos||01-11-2001||02:59 AM||||Ok... Lets see it from the speakers point of view.<P>A speaker has a negative terminal whitch is kept at a constant level, I think you'll agree with that. The positive terminal varies between a positive and a negative voltage compared to the negative. Therefor the currente must flow both ways through the speaker and thereby creating a forward AND backward motion of the coil/cone. and if a current flows both ways you have and alternating current. You know this ofcours but keep in mind that the current always flows from the + to -. and when the potential of the + terminal is below that of the - terminal it in fact has a lower potential than the - terminal allthough it is still called +.  Come on you know I'm right  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000023||Oreif||01-11-2001||10:27 AM||||The current does not alternate directions. The direction of the current flow controls the drive direction of the speaker. As an experiment, take a large woofer. Apply a 10hz signal to the inputs. You will notice the speaker pushes outward at the peak of the sinewave. Now revese the wires (changing direction of current flow) You'll notice that at the peak of the 10hz sinewave the speaker cone is now being pulled in. (at the bottom of the sinewave the speaker cone is near it's static state in either case above) The signal out of the amp is still at the same voltage levels and the voice coil is just that, a coil, so it is not a polarized electrically (like a diode). An electromagnets magnetic poles are controlled by direction of current.<BR>If the signal was an AC signal then it would not matter which way you hooked it up (+/- or -/+) the speakers would still be in phase and that is not true. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 01-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||Rednax Asbestos||01-11-2001||01:56 PM||||ok here we go..<P>Lets say a signal (sinewave) starts out at 0 volts. it will steadilly rise to it's peak, lets say about 10 volts. this voltage in its turn will create an magnetic field in the coil. This field will repel the field of the static magnet and thus move the cone outward. After that the voltage will steadilly return to its original position namely 0 volt. And then comes the second part of the sinewave. It will steadilly go to - 10 volts and this will create a current in the opposite direction in the coil. this will lead to the magnetic field in the coil whitch will attract to the magnet. and therefor move the cone inward.  after that its back to 0 volt and the whole will start over again. the 0 volt is the "rest"-state of the woofer. and according to the DIRECTION of the current it will move inward or out. <BR>Now phase...<P>If a speaker is wired the wrong way around it will move inward when it should move outward. and this is exactly what hapens if you reverse polarity.<P>back to you.||||reg||1
